1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,345
QIQ, qweninqwen.

2
00:00:01,345 --> 00:00:06,600
One real Qwen three zero point six B token, computed by a processor that lives inside the

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:09,198
weights of a modified Qwen three point five.

4
00:00:09,198 --> 00:00:12,918
This is a short tour of what the repository's own results show.

5
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:18,939
Qwenputer showed that a RISC-V processor can be compiled into a model's weights and run as the

6
00:00:18,939 --> 00:00:20,167
model generates tokens.

7
00:00:20,167 --> 00:00:25,186
The question here: can that processor run another language model, a real one, with the weights

8
00:00:25,186 --> 00:00:30,152
fetched from the outer model's own parameters, and the host doing nothing but looping tokens?

9
00:00:31,150 --> 00:00:36,497
The inner model is real Qwen three zero point six B, as a C firmware with int four weights and

10
00:00:36,497 --> 00:00:38,374
three layers at higher precision.

11
00:00:38,374 --> 00:00:42,128
It runs on the circuit, one instruction per generated trace token.

12
00:00:42,128 --> 00:00:47,191
Its weights arrive as ROW fetches from the outer model's embedding table, and its working

13
00:00:47,191 --> 00:00:48,784
memory is circuit owned RAM.

14
00:00:48,784 --> 00:00:50,832
It ends by emitting its answer word.

15
00:00:51,830 --> 00:00:59,140
The outer model keeps the Qwen three point five layer design, widened to hidden size 7,680

16
00:00:59,140 --> 00:01:04,663
with twelve added chip layers and an extended vocabulary of 680,128.

17
00:01:04,663 --> 00:01:10,511
The checkpoint holds 168,684 table rows, and the host only loops tokens.

18
00:01:10,511 --> 00:01:15,465
Across the whole run it injected 0 weight, RAM or blob words.

19
00:01:16,470 --> 00:01:17,259
The result.

20
00:01:17,259 --> 00:01:23,713
For the prompt token seven eighty five, the circuit halts on its own with the word 15,846.

21
00:01:23,713 --> 00:01:30,096
That is exactly the token Hugging Face's Qwen three zero point six B produces, in both fp

22
00:01:30,096 --> 00:01:31,961
thirty two and bf sixteen.

23
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:40,966
The run was split into 96 chunks, and all 96 came back clean: 0 free token mismatches, 0 forced

24
00:01:40,966 --> 00:01:43,494
tokens, and 0 host injections.

25
00:01:43,494 --> 00:01:48,466
About 199.3 million trace tokens, and 12.1 of GPU job time.

26
00:01:49,470 --> 00:01:52,430
Each bar is one chunk, in minutes.

27
00:01:52,430 --> 00:01:58,090
The last chunk is short because the circuit halts at step 57,705.

28
00:01:58,090 --> 00:02:05,317
Chunks ran on rented RTX forty nineties, A100s and an L40S, plus 39 on Modal A100s.

29
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:13,112
An RTX 4090 took about 5.8 per chunk on average across 50 chunks, a Modal A100 about 9.6.

30
00:02:14,110 --> 00:02:15,948
Chunks are stitched at seams.

31
00:02:15,948 --> 00:02:21,970
All 95 of 95 seams are exact: registers, program counter, all eight thousand one hundred ninety

32
00:02:21,970 --> 00:02:23,744
two RAM words and the latch.

33
00:02:23,744 --> 00:02:26,597
Chunk zero boots from the firmware ELF alone.

34
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:33,160
As a separate check, the merged checkpoint was run through Hugging Face's stock Qwen three

35
00:02:33,160 --> 00:02:35,632
point five module classes on chunk zero.

36
00:02:35,632 --> 00:02:41,315
Over 130,000 steps, with the argmax taken over the full vocabulary, there were 0 mismatches.

37
00:02:41,315 --> 00:02:47,123
As a negative control, we shifted every table entry by one, and it fails 20 times, so the pass

38
00:02:47,123 --> 00:02:50,644
depends on the table held in the checkpoint's parameters.

39
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:52,799
Scope and honesty.

40
00:02:52,799 --> 00:02:58,659
This is one prompt token and one output token, so it is a feasibility result, not a serving

41
00:02:58,659 --> 00:02:59,110
system.

42
00:02:59,110 --> 00:03:05,163
The firmware is quantized, its logits have a cosine of 0.917 with Hugging Face, though its top

43
00:03:05,163 --> 00:03:06,901
one margin is wide at 0.97.

44
00:03:06,901 --> 00:03:10,894
And the run took about 12.1 of GPU time for that single token.

45
00:03:10,894 --> 00:03:13,856
The host's exact roles are listed in the repo.

46
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:19,754
The verdict file, the seam report, and the host role audit are all in the repository.

